# An Interactive Journey Through Min-Plus Collision Geometry

A min-plus digest records the cheapest adjusted coordinate under each key. Begin with the central experiment: move one coordinate indefinitely while watching every digest component remain fixed.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The phenomenon is guaranteed whenever the message dimension $k$ exceeds the number of outputs $r$. Each output selects one minimizing witness, and fewer than $k$ witnesses cannot cover all $k$ coordinates.

{{visualization:0}}

## Find the escape route yourself

Use the witness explorer to see the pigeonhole principle become geometric. Red coordinates certify outputs; any green coordinate can be increased without disturbing those certificates.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the complete theorem and proof idea</summary>

**Universal Collision-Ray Theorem.** For arbitrary $r$ min-plus keys on $k$ real coordinates with $r<k$, every message $m$ has a coordinate $q$ such that $D(m+d e_q)=D(m)$ for all $d\ge 0$.

Choose one minimizing coordinate for each output. Their range contains at most $r$ coordinates, so a coordinate $q$ lies outside it. Every chosen minimum remains available after increasing $q$, and the changed candidate can only increase. Hence all output minima remain unchanged.

</details>

## From one collision to a continuum

Positive increments change the message. Distinct increments change the escaping coordinate by distinct amounts, so the ray is injectively parameterized.

